The purpose of fasting is to **draw closer to Heavenly Father and Jesus Christ through humble worship, prayer, and dependence on Them**. Fasting is more than going without food and drink; it helps us turn our hearts toward God and seek His guidance, strength, and blessings.

In October 2026 General Conference, Elder Ulisses Soares taught that fasting is **“an act of worship that shapes and refines our discipleship and draws us closer to Him.”** He explained that fasting joined with prayer deepens our faith and devotion, makes us more aware of our dependence on the Lord, and prepares us to receive personal revelation. ([churchofjesuschrist.org](https://www.churchofjesuschrist.org/study/general-conference/2026/10/17soares?lang=eng&utm_source=openai))

Fasting can therefore help us:

- humble ourselves before God;
- strengthen our ability to resist temptation and overcome weaknesses;
- seek revelation, comfort, or guidance;
- pray for the needs of others; and
- develop greater charity and Christlike compassion.

The fast offering is also part of the law of the fast. Elder Soares taught that fasting should turn our hearts toward God’s children, while our generous offerings help “the oppressed go free” and bless those in need. ([churchofjesuschrist.org](https://www.churchofjesuschrist.org/study/general-conference/2026/10/17soares?lang=eng&utm_source=openai))

In summary, we fast not merely to feel hunger, but to **worship God, align our desires with His will, receive spiritual strength, and bless others**. As Elder Soares said, fasting with prayer and generosity brings “sweeter communion with the Lord.” ([churchofjesuschrist.org](https://www.churchofjesuschrist.org/study/general-conference/2026/10/17soares?lang=eng&utm_source=openai))
